    whyItMatters: "Gene expression explains the difference between genotype and phenotype at the molecular level. In the HSC, understanding gene expression is key to explaining how identical DNA sequences can produce different cell types (e.g., nerve cells vs. muscle cells) and how environmental and epigenetic factors influence which genes are turned on or off.",
    scenario: "A researcher was puzzled by how all cells in the human body contain identical DNA, yet liver cells, skin cells, and neurones look and function so differently. Investigation revealed that different sets of genes were expressed in different cell types — liver cells expressed genes for metabolic enzymes while skin cells expressed genes for keratin. This selective gene expression, governed by transcription factors and epigenetic modifications, explained the specialisation of cells during development.",
    example: '"Even though every cell in your body has the same DNA, gene expression differs — that\'s why a muscle cell behaves so differently from a brain cell."',
    related: ["Transcription", "Translation", "Epigenetics", "DNA Replication", "Phenotype"],
