    definition: "Epigenetics is the study of heritable changes in gene expression that do not involve changes to the DNA sequence itself. Epigenetic mechanisms include DNA methylation (which silences genes) and histone modification (which affects how tightly DNA is packaged). Environmental factors such as diet, stress, and toxins can alter epigenetic patterns.",
    whyItMatters: "Epigenetics challenges the traditional view that only DNA sequence determines inheritance. In the HSC, epigenetics appears in discussions of gene expression, environmental influences on phenotype, and non-traditional inheritance patterns. It also has implications for understanding cancer and developmental biology.",
    scenario: "A researcher studied identical twins who had developed different health outcomes — one had developed type 2 diabetes while the other had not, despite identical DNA. Analysis revealed significant differences in the methylation patterns of genes involved in insulin regulation between the twins. These epigenetic differences had accumulated over decades of different lifestyles, diet, and stress exposure, demonstrating how the environment can influence gene expression without changing the underlying DNA sequence.",
    example: '"Epigenetic changes caused by early childhood stress can alter gene expression patterns that persist into adulthood, influencing susceptibility to depression and anxiety."',
