    whyItMatters: "PCR makes it possible to analyse tiny amounts of DNA — from a single hair, a drop of blood, or a partially degraded ancient specimen. In the HSC, PCR is a prerequisite for gel electrophoresis and DNA profiling, both of which appear as short-answer and extended-response questions.",
    scenario: "A forensic scientist received a blood sample from a crime scene that was too small to analyse directly. Using PCR, the scientist amplified the DNA in the sample through 30 cycles of heating and cooling, producing over a billion copies of the target region. The amplified DNA was then separated by gel electrophoresis to produce a DNA profile, which was compared to samples from suspects and used as evidence in the subsequent trial.",
