    definition: "B lymphocytes (B cells) are white blood cells produced in bone marrow that are responsible for antibody-mediated (humoral) immunity. When a B cell encounters its specific antigen, it differentiates into plasma cells that produce and secrete antibodies, and memory B cells that persist long-term for a rapid secondary response.",
    whyItMatters: "B lymphocytes are the cells responsible for producing antibodies — making them central to the adaptive immune response. In the HSC, distinguishing B cell from T cell functions and understanding the clonal selection and clonal expansion of B cells is necessary for explaining both immunity and vaccination.",
    scenario: "A student traced the activation of B lymphocytes after a person received a tetanus vaccine. The vaccine introduced tetanus toxoid antigens into the bloodstream. Circulating B lymphocytes with receptors complementary to the tetanus antigen were selected and activated — they proliferated rapidly (clonal expansion) to produce large numbers of plasma cells that secreted tetanus-specific antibodies. Long-lived memory B cells remained in the blood, ready to produce antibodies rapidly if tetanus toxin was encountered in the future.",
    example: '"B cells are the antibody factories of the immune system — once they recognise the antigen, they clone themselves and start producing vast quantities of specific antibodies."',
