    level: "Advanced",
    definition: "Koch's Postulates are a set of four criteria established by Robert Koch in the 19th century to determine whether a specific microorganism causes a particular disease. The postulates require that the organism be found in all cases of the disease, be isolated and cultured, cause the disease when introduced to a healthy host, and be re-isolated from the newly infected host.",
    whyItMatters: "Koch's Postulates provided the scientific framework for identifying causative agents of infectious disease and remain foundational in medical microbiology. In the HSC, they are used to illustrate the process of scientific reasoning and to discuss cases where the postulates cannot be fully applied — such as for viruses that cannot be cultured outside host cells.",
    scenario: "A student applied Koch's Postulates to analyse a historical case study involving cholera. Robert Koch had isolated Vibrio cholerae from cholera victims, grown pure cultures of the bacterium, introduced the cultures into healthy animals which subsequently developed cholera-like symptoms, and re-isolated the same bacterium from the newly infected animals. Each step of the postulates was satisfied, establishing V. cholerae as the causative agent of cholera and providing a model for investigating all subsequent infectious diseases.",
    example: '"Koch\'s Postulates gave us a rigorous method for proving disease causation — before them, there was no systematic way to link a specific microorganism to a specific disease."',
